  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1209 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nOur pleasant weather continues as northerly flow is seen in the \nlower and middle parts of the atmosphere leading to a continued \ndrying out across our area Tuesday night. This drier air will result \nin a much more refreshing airmass then we have seen the past few \ndays with cooler mornings and milder afternoons along with much \nlower humidity. Daily highs should stay in the mid to upper 80s for \nafternoon highs with the morning lows dipping down into the upper \n50s across the Hill Country and into the low to mid 60s elsewhere. \nThis begins to change by late week as a possible disturbance tries \nestablishing itself later this week across West Texas through the \nConcho Valley. This disturbance may be enough to bring some low end \nrain chances (~15-25%) across the region. Additionally, this mid-\nlevel disturbance could be aided by some influence and moisture from \nthe development of a potential surface low that is projected to move \nover the Bay of Campeche. The National Hurricane Center is currently \nmonitoring this potential and has provided up to a medium (50%) \nchance of development into a Tropical Depression or Storm over the\nnext 7 days. Current steering flows suggest that this disturbance\nmoves north northeastward across the Gulf. This would allow our \narea to remain on the drier side with only temperatures really \nbeing the major influence from this system. For now it is still a \nbit too far out to put any confidence in a particular model \nsolution. However, we will continue to keep an eye on this \ndisturbance and watch to see how this unfolds and what impacts if \nany this would bring to our local area.",
